From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 11:58:09 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] v4l2: size the decoder's input blocks from the device's coded
 buffer size

The decoder asked the framework for input blocks of 16 MiB, and 64 MiB
above 1080p. The framework allocates every input block at that size and a
stream keeps several of them, so a 4K stream held about 0.9 GB of input
memory, while the component copies only the bytes a frame uses and the
device takes coded buffers of 8 MiB.

Read the size of the coded buffers the device negotiates from its input
queue when the codec is listed (a TRY_FMT with no size requested, which
the driver raises to the largest packet it takes), and make that the most
an input block is asked to be, within 1 MiB and 16 MiB. A device that
states none gets 16 MiB. The default maximum input size follows the
picture size up to that: 1 MiB up to 1280x720, 2 MiB up to 1920x1088,
4 MiB up to 4096x2304 and the device's size above, the sizes the device
states once a client gives it the picture size. A stream that keeps eight
blocks held 64 MiB of them at any size, 32 MiB at 4K now and 16 MiB at
1080p.

The decoder states the same picture size with the coded format it sets on
the device. It stated none: ui::Size() is -1 x -1, which the driver clamps
to the largest picture the device decodes, and for that the device asks
for its largest packet, 8 MiB, so the first coded buffer of a block
smaller than that was refused (V4L2 QBUF failed, error 14). The size is
the interface's picture size, the one the blocks follow.

The maximum input size is never below that size for the picture size,
whatever a client states: a client states the largest sample of the clip
it starts with (a few tens of KB in CTS AdaptivePlaybackTest) and the
stream carries larger ones later. The old calculator raised the value for
the same reason. The setting of the device's size is read-only and the
component's own, and the setter reads it and the picture size. A sample
larger than the blocks is the client's to split or refuse. The decoder
logs a device that needs more than the blocks it has.
